{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjX5kBQyg78Y9e5tMzqb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BBrnXyfwPnPDwmR5rdXjz5","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with a G clef and a one-sharp key signature. Next comes a rest. Then a sharp, a two-note 32nd figure with the upper note on C#5, and four repeated 16ths on B4 (the middle line). That is the second half of bar 56: rest, (B4 C#5 32nds), then four B4 16ths. So the line starts partway through bar 56, which the previous line began. After the first barline come two groups of six 16ths. The first starts on B4 and steps down to the F#4 repeats, with the little G#4 figure. The second starts on F#4 and drops to repeated D#4, with a sharp shown. That is bar 57. After the second barline come six 16ths, from D#4 down to repeated B3, then a quarter on B3 and a rest under a fermata. That is bar 58. A double bar follows, with \"Da Capo il Minuetto e il Larghetto\" and nothing further. The only notation difference is the opening rest, which the copyist draws more simply than the edition's 16th rest. Everything else matches in pitch and rhythm. So the line holds bars 56–58, not 57–58.","right":false,"insights":"The tail of bar 56 at the start of the line is short and easy to miss. Its repeated B4 16ths look like the start of bar 57, so the program skipped it. Checking the first notes against the end of the earlier bar would have caught it: the sharped C#5 32nd pair is unique to bar 56. A rest before the first barline is also a clue that the line starts partway through a bar."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5285,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5285},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"sta